---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Arms from "../components/Arms.astro";
import FamilyArms from "../components/FamilyArms.astro";
import Flags from "../components/Flags.astro";
---
<Base title="Arms & Flags" eyebrow="Evidence, not a badge"
      dek="This is the part of family history where the internet lies to people. Here is the honest position.">
<section class="blk">
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>There is no coat of arms for a surname</h3>
    <p>Arms belong to a person and their descendants, granted or assumed. They never belong to everyone who happens to share a name. The sites that sell “the De Franceschi family crest” are selling a picture keyed to a text string, and putting one on a family homepage is the fastest way to make a serious archive look unserious.</p>
    <p>It is worth adding what Carlo De Franceschi's own biographer wrote of his origins at Moncalvo: <em>da modesti possidenti</em> — of modest landowners. Not gentry. Not armigerous. That one phrase is better evidence about this family than any armorial.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Except that this family did have arms — and said so</h2>
  <p class="dek">Everything below about surname-crests still stands. But in a private letter of about 1873, Carlo De Franceschi blazoned his own.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px;align-items:start">
    <FamilyArms />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>He is writing to Giovanni Kobler about the Defranceschi families of Fiume, working out which of them are his relatives. Having placed them, he adds a sentence about the arms — and then, characteristically, admits the limit of his own knowledge: <em>“Non so se lo conservino i discendenti di Giambattista, e se n'abbiano alcuno quelli che provengono da Valentino”</em> — I do not know whether Giambattista's descendants still keep it, or whether those who come from Valentino have any.</p>
      <p>Two things make this worth more than a catalogue entry. It is <strong>first-person</strong>: the head of the Gologorica house describing what his family used, not a vendor matching a text string. And it <strong>matches nothing</strong> in the armorials — no pine, no lions, no bend. A Greek cross, two arrows and a pomegranate is not what you would invent if you were reaching for a generic Italian achievement.</p>
      <p>He adds the family's account of why they might have borne arms at all: they believed they had belonged in Carnia to the patriarchal nobility called <em>dei Germani</em> — a standing, he notes, that the Venetian government did not recognise after it took that country. <span class="chip inf" style="padding:2px 7px;font-size:10px">Inferred</span> — that part is tradition, and he reports it as such.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:28px">Read this against the section below, which was written before the letter was found. The general point holds absolutely: there is no coat of arms for a surname, and the achievements sold under this name belong to other families. What has changed is that <em>this</em> family, at Gologorica, is now on record describing its own — and it is none of them.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the armorials record instead</h2>
  <p class="dek">Three achievements are genuinely documented for families named De Franceschi — most fully for the Corsican line of Florentine origin. None has been shown to belong to the Istrian family.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:26px"><Arms /></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">The blazon is the record — arms are defined by their words, and any two emblazonments of the same blazon differ in the drawing. So the fields, divisions, ordinaries and the tree are rendered faithfully here, and the lions are named rather than drawn. An invented lion would add nothing true to the description and would quietly become the thing people copied. Naming them keeps the page honest about where the evidence actually is.</div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The flags, by contrast, are certain</h2>
  <p class="dek">We know exactly which standard flew over each village in any given year, because the sovereignty chart establishes it. So the heraldry that <em>is</em> honest here is civic — and every person in the archive can carry the flag they were actually born under.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:26px"><Flags /></div>
  <p class="cap">Schematic renderings: field divisions and principal charges only. For the pre-1420 period the arms of Aquileia, the counts of Gorizia and the Frankopan lords vary by context and are not reduced to a swatch here.</p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two things worth looking for</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>First, the 1711 house on the Gologorica square. Istrian lintels and doorheads frequently carry a date and a house mark or arms; somebody should photograph it properly. Second, the <em>cramars</em> and merchant families of Carnia used house marks — non-noble identifying signs — which appear on contracts and grave slabs. If this family has an authentic emblem, that is where it is, not in a heraldry catalogue.</p>
    <p>And if a modern emblem is wanted anyway: commission one. Neither Italy nor Croatia regulates private assumption of arms, so a new emblem for the living family is legitimate — provided the site says plainly that it was made in the 2020s and inherits nothing. Designed from real material — the pine of the Carnian slopes, the Vinodol wave, a Glagolitic letter from the Gračišće graffiti — it would be honest and genuinely yours.</p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
